## Detection Rules

### Critical: Naming Conventions
| Pattern | Issue | Severity |
|---------|-------|----------|
| `snake_case` variables | Inconsistent with TypeScript camelCase | CRITICAL |
| Mixed case in same file | Hard to read | CRITICAL |
| Interface not PascalCase | Not following convention | HIGH |
| Type alias not PascalCase | Not following convention | HIGH |
| Function names not camelCase | Inconsistent | HIGH |
| Variable names too short (single letter) | Not descriptive | HIGH |
| Constant names in lowerCase | Should be `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` or `camelCase` depending on convention | MEDIUM |

```typescript
// BAD: snake_case variable
const user_name: string = 'John';

// GOOD: camelCase variable
const userName: string = 'John';

// BAD: Interface not PascalCase
interface user_data {
  id: string;
  name: string;
}

// GOOD: Interface PascalCase
interface UserData {
  id: string;
  name: string;
}

// BAD: Type alias not PascalCase
type user_list = UserData[];

// GOOD: Type alias PascalCase
type UserList = UserData[];

// BAD: Short variable name
const u = 'John'; // What is 'u'?

// GOOD: Descriptive name
const userName = 'John';

// BAD: Function not camelCase
function GetUser(id: string): UserData { ... }

// GOOD: Function camelCase
function getUser(id: string): UserData { ... }
```

### High: File and Folder Structure
| Pattern | Issue | Severity |
|---------|-------|----------|
| `index.ts` not used for barrel exports | Redundant imports | HIGH |
| Mixed relative/absolute imports | Inconsistent | HIGH |
| Deep relative paths (`../../..`) | Hard to refactor | HIGH |
| Missing `.js` extension in imports (if `moduleResolution: node16+`) | Causes errors | HIGH |
| `import type` not used for type-only imports | Unnecessary runtime overhead | MEDIUM |
| Cyclic imports | Creates bundle issues | CRITICAL |

```typescript
// BAD: No index.ts barrel
// user.ts, formatUser.ts, types.ts in same folder
import { fetchUser } from './fetchUser';
import { formatUser } from './formatUser';
import { User } from './types';

// GOOD: Barrel export in index.ts
// export { fetchUser } from './fetchUser';
// export { formatUser } from './formatUser';
// export type { User } from './types';
import { fetchUser, formatUser, type User } from './utils';

// BAD: Deep relative path
import { api } from '../../../../api/config';

// GOOD: Use alias (if configured) or restructure
import { api } from '@app/api/config';

// BAD: Non-type import for type
import { User } from './types'; // User is only used as type

// GOOD: Type import
import type { User } from './types';
```

### Medium: Comment and Documentation Style
| Pattern | Issue | Severity |
|---------|-------|----------|
| Inline comments inconsistent (`//` vs `/* */`) | Style mismatch | MEDIUM |
| Missing JSDoc on exported functions | Poor maintainability | HIGH |
| JSDoc incomplete (no @param, @returns) | Hard to understand | MEDIUM |
| TODO/FIXME without issue reference | Unactionable | LOW |
| Comment style not matching project standard | Consistency concern | LOW |

```typescript
// BAD: Missing JSDoc on exported function
export function fetchUser(id: string) {
  return api.get(`/users/${id}`);
}

// GOOD: Proper JSDoc
/**
 * Fetches a user by ID.
 * @param id - The unique identifier of the user
 * @returns The user data
 */
export function fetchUser(id: string): Promise<User> {
  return api.get(`/users/${id}`);
}

// BAD: Inconsistent comment style
// This is a line comment
/* This is a block comment */ // mixed

// GOOD: Consistent – use // for single-line, /* */ for multi-line
// This is a single-line comment
/*
 * This is a
 * multi-line comment
 */

// BAD: TODO without tracking
// TODO: add error handling

// GOOD: TODO with reference
// TODO: add error handling (tracked in JIRA-456)
```

### Medium: Import Order and Grouping
| Pattern | Issue | Severity |
|---------|-------|----------|
| No separation of external/internal imports | Hard to scan | MEDIUM |
| Mixed default and named imports without convention | Inconsistent | LOW |
| Imports not alphabetically sorted | Hard to find | MEDIUM |

```typescript
// BAD: Mixed imports without order
import { api } from '../api';
import { z } from 'zod';
import React from 'react';
import { User } from './types';

// GOOD: Sorted and grouped
// External
import React from 'react';
import { z } from 'zod';
// Internal
import { api } from '../api';
import { User } from './types';

// BAD: Alphabetically unsorted
import { z } from 'zod';
import { api } from '../api';
import React from 'react';

// GOOD: Sorted
import React from 'react';
import { z } from 'zod';
import { api } from '../api';
```

### Medium: Formatting (Indentation, Line Length, Semicolons)
| Pattern | Issue | Severity |
|---------|-------|----------|
| Mixed tabs and spaces | Inconsistent indentation | HIGH |
| Line length > 120 characters | Hard to read | MEDIUM |
| Semicolon usage inconsistent | Style mismatch | LOW |
| Trailing whitespace | Unnecessary diff noise | LOW |
| Missing trailing newline | Some linters require it | LOW |

```typescript
// BAD: Mixed indentation (tab + spaces)
    function test() {
    return 1;
}

// GOOD: Consistent spaces (2 or 4)
function test() {
  return 1;
}

// BAD: Long line
const result = await api.get(`/users/${userId}/posts/${postId}/comments/${commentId}`);

// GOOD: Break into multiple lines
const result = await api.get(
  `/users/${userId}/posts/${postId}/comments/${commentId}`
);

// BAD: Inconsistent semicolons
const a = 1
const b = 2;

// GOOD: Consistent – choose one (prefer always semicolons)
const a = 1;
const b = 2;
```

### Low: TypeScript-Specific Patterns
| Pattern | Recommendation | Severity |
|---------|----------------|----------|
| Using `type` vs `interface` inconsistently | Choose one convention | LOW |
| Using `enum` where string union would work | Consider union for simpler types | LOW |
| Namespace usage in modern TypeScript | Prefer modules | LOW |

```typescript
// BAD: Mixed type/interface for similar shapes
interface User { id: string; }
type Admin = { id: string; permissions: string[]; };

// GOOD: Consistent – use interface for objects, type for unions
interface User { id: string; }
interface Admin extends User { permissions: string[]; }

// Or use type everywhere
type User = { id: string; };
type Admin = { id: string; permissions: string[]; };

// BAD: Enum for simple string values
enum Status {
  Active = 'active',
  Inactive = 'inactive',
}

// GOOD: String union (simpler)
type Status = 'active' | 'inactive';

// BAD: Namespace (legacy)
namespace MyLib {
  export function doSomething(): void {}
}

// GOOD: Module (modern)
export function doSomething(): void {}
```

### tsconfig.json Style Related Options
| Option | Recommended | Severity |
|--------|-------------|---------|
| `noUnusedLocals: false` | Enable to keep code clean | HIGH |
| `noUnusedParameters: false` | Enable (prefix unused with underscore) | HIGH |
| `noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature: false` | Not required but helps consistency | LOW |
| `include`/`exclude` too broad | May cause slow type checking | MEDIUM |

```json
